{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Lie Detectors"; alternate title: "I Kid You Not|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1963|02|24}} |ProdCode=8 |ShortSummary=
:Host is Dr. John Rich, a psychiatrist with the [[University of Toronto]] and [[Queen's University at Kingston|Queen's University]]. This program examines the [[autonomic nervous system]], how it works, and what it can reveal.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42953820/ |title=CBOT Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=February 23, 1963 |page=3, The TV Journal |access-date=December 7, 2016 |archive-date=December 20, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220111208/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42953820/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Smoking and [[Lung Cancer]]|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1963|03|03}} |ProdCode=9}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Science Museums|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1963|03|10}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:To commemorate the [[Canadian Centennial]] in 1967 it has been proposed that Canada build a [[Canada Science and Technology Museum|national museum of science]]. The program includes filmed demonstrations of how science and technology can be made meaningful to the general public.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42425196/ |title=CBOT Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=March 9, 1963 |page=3, The TV Journal |access-date=December 7, 2016 |archive-date=December 20, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220120107/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42425196/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Count on Me |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1963|03|17}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:Drs. [[Patterson Hume]] and [[Donald Ivey]] of the [[University of Toronto]] explain the history and operation of what were once called calculating machines and are now known as [[computers]]. The program emphasizes how computers store information, produce it on demand, and use the offered information in the best possible way.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42425809/ |title=TV Programs and highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=March 16, 1963 |page=3, The TV Journal |access-date=April 1, 2016 |archive-date=April 16, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160416140005/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/42425809/ |url-status=live }}</ref> (repeat from 07/12/1962)}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tornadoes|OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1963|03|24}}|ProdCode=12}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Descent of Man |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1963|03|31}}|ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:Recent fossil discoveries in Africa have shed new light on the ancestry and [[evolution of man]]. Guest Dr. [[L.S.B. Leakey]], renowned British anthropologist and paleontologist, unearthed fossil remains in the [[Olduvai Gorge]] that have extended the time scale of human evolution from 500,000 to two million years or more. A deductive story in [[anthropology]] and [[paleontology]] is told as Dr. Leakey describes his finds and interprets their significance.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=OKUtAAAAIBAJ&pg=5206%2C6006381 |title=Weekend TV Previews |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=March 30, 1963 |page=13 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123809/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=OKUtAAAAIBAJ&pg=5206,6006381 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

