{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Last Stand: Western Mountain Parks|OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1970|11|02}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:The first in a four-part series entitled ''The Last Stand.'' The series looks at a variety of areas in the world set aside as specially protected areas of wilderness and natural wildlife. The first program is about western mountain parks and the work being done by biologists and scientists to save mountain wildlife.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HZIuAAAAIBAJ&pg=1386%2C8796513 |title=HIGHLIGHTS this week |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=October 31, 1970 |pages=3, 13 |access-date=2016-04-08 |archive-date=2023-10-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123738/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HZIuAAAAIBAJ&pg=1386,8796513 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8-JUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1141%2C43707 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=November 2, 1970 |page=7 |access-date=2016-04-08 |archive-date=2023-10-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123815/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=8-JUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1141,43707 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Last Stand: The Everglades of Florida |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1970|11|09}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:The [[Everglades]], unique in the world, are dependent entirely on water. But the beautiful birds and animals in the park are threatened by land development and a new airport, whose drainage policies are drying up the area.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46007211/ |title=TV Programs and Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=November 9, 1970 |page=30 |access-date=2016-04-29 |archive-date=2016-05-31 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160531070047/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46007211/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Last Stand: Point Pelee|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1970|11|16}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:The third in a four-part series entitled "The Last Stand." [[Point Pelee National Park|Point Pelee]] is a tiny peninsula in southwestern Ontario, jutting into Lake Erie, which contains a fresh water marsh full of wildlife of all kinds. It is also the last stronghold of the southern deciduous forest in Canada and contains southern species of plants and animals not found anywhere else in the country.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46019542/ |title=TV Programs and Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=November 16, 1970 |page=26 |access-date=May 14, 2016 |archive-date=June 4, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160604184010/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46019542/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Last Stand: The Southwestern Desert|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1970|11|23}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:The last in a four-part series entitled The Last Stand. This program looks at the [[Sonoran Desert]] in the U.S. Southwest and in Mexico. It contains an enormous variety of animal life and represents adaptation by both plant and animal life to a harsh environment where competition is keen and only the most successful can survive.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=q8gyAAAAIBAJ&pg=2072%2C2361869 |title=Mon. / Nov. 23 night |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 21, 1970 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123655/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=q8gyAAAAIBAJ&pg=2072,2361869 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Sense of Time: The Age of the Universe|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1970|12|07}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:The first in a three-part series entitled "A Sense of Time". This episode examines past and present ideas on the questions of how old is the universe.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/48193948/ |title=TV Programs and Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=December 7, 1970 |page=22 |access-date=May 15, 2016 |archive-date=June 5, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160605053627/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/48193948/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BuVUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1159%2C1391920 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=December 7, 1970 |page=7 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123755/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BuVUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1159,1391920 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Sense of Time: The Age of the Earth|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1970|12|14}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:This program focuses on a new geophysical concept of our planet.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=C-VUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1167%2C2631895 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=December 14, 1970 |page=7 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123740/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=C-VUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1167,2631895 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Sense of Time: The Age of Man|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1970|12|21}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:Planet Earth has supported life for some three billion years; but Man, characterized by his powers of thought and other intelligent faculties, has shown the greatest development during his 500,000-years existence. Can he assume his role of responsibility to protect his life-giving biosphere?<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=kr0yAAAAIBAJ&pg=654%2C1416591 |title=Mon. / Dec. 21 night |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=December 19, 1970 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123649/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=kr0yAAAAIBAJ&pg=654,1416591 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Celebration of Swans |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1970|12|28}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Life cycle of a pair of rare trumpeter swans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98937378/ |title=Tonight's Television Programs |work=Detroit Free Press |date=December 28, 1970 |access-date=May 27, 2016 |archive-date=June 30, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160630183234/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98937378/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Breath of Life |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|01|25}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Every year, over 12,000 Canadians are born with serious inherited defects. This program focuses on treatment of one young victim of cystic fibrosis.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_44zAAAAIBAJ&pg=3710%2C5600815 |title=HIGHLIGHTS This Week |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=January 23, 1971 |page=3, TV section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123650/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_44zAAAAIBAJ&pg=3710,5600815 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_44zAAAAIBAJ&pg=4945%2C5655713 |title=Mon. / Jan. 25 |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=January 23, 1971 |page=13, TV section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123701/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=_44zAAAAIBAJ&pg=4945,5655713 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Lakes (Part I) |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|02|01}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:Sociologists tell us that the Great Lakes are the basis for the civilization around them. If the lakes fail, so will we. The program explores the concept that we must cease to think of land and water as separate worlds, and instead treat them as a unity with an international plan for management.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BI8zAAAAIBAJ&pg=2131%2C7343009 |title=Mon. / Feb. 1 |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=January 30, 1971 |page=13, TV section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123657/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BI8zAAAAIBAJ&pg=2131,7343009 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Lakes (Conclusion) |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|02|08}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:Immediate implementation of pollution control in our Great Lakes is urgently needed if we are to preserve our most vital waterway. But what are the implications of such action?<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=sq4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=5306%2C1367879 |title=Mon. / Feb. 8 night |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=February 6, 1971 |page=13, TV section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123704/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=sq4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=5306,1367879 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Population Problems: Everybody's Baby (Part I) |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|02|15}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:An exploration of the most difficult and serious issue threatening mankind today, the population crisis.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=uK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=4959%2C3308115 |title=Mon. / Feb. 15 night |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=February 13, 1971 |page=13, TV section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123648/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=uK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=4959,3308115 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Population Problems: Tomorrow's Child |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|03|01}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:Featuring a national opinion poll on public attitudes in Canada towards population growth.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=5035%2C6883991 |title=Mon. / Mar. 1 night |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=February 27, 1971 |page=13, tv times |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123644/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=5035,6883991 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Living Arctic |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|03|03}} |ProdCode=special |ShortSummary=
:A two hour documentary on the Canadian North, its majestic wildlife and its increasing exploitation by man for natural resources.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=3713%2C6938595 |title=The Living Arctic |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=February 27, 1971 |page=24, tv times |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123658/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xK4tAAAAIBAJ&pg=3713,6938595 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Psychiatry: Who Help Themselves |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|03|15}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:Dealing with the McGill University Settlement Mental Health Unit project in Montreal.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=vZgkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5227%2C3484825 |title=Mon. / Mar. 15 night |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=March 13, 1971 |page=13, tv times |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123705/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=vZgkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5227,3484825 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Psychiatry: Heavy Night |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|03|22}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:Second in a series on the development and potential of psychiatry for the masses, focusing on informal youth clinics established in a low-income area of Montreal.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98944282/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=March 21, 1971 |page=15-D |access-date=June 12, 2016 |archive-date=August 10, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160810065913/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98944282/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Psychiatry: Street Fighting Mad |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|03|29}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:A visit to Montreal's [[Allan Memorial Institute]], where a disturbed teenager responds to treatment.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98946430/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=March 28, 1971 |page=14-D |access-date=June 14, 2016 |archive-date=August 11, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160811073640/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98946430/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Psychiatry: Human Potential |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1971|04|05}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
