{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Vanishing Peoples: Lacandons, The Mayas of Mexico |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1972|11|06}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season opener: The [[Lacandon people|Lacandons]], the last surviving descendants of the Mayas, live in the rain forest of southern Mexico and cling to ancient beliefs and traditions. Narration is by Mia Anderson and the voice of Chan K'in is by [[Chief Dan George]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=i_JUAAAAIBAJ&pg=972%2C1110700 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=November 6, 1972 |page=7 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123654/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=i_JUAAAAIBAJ&pg=972,1110700 |url-status=live }}</ref> }}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Sexes, part 1 of 2 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1972|11|20}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:Examines male and female roles in society and presents a scientific study of the known biological facts about sex differences in humans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=k_JUAAAAIBAJ&pg=989%2C4169968 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=November 20, 1972 |page=7}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Sexes, part 2 of 2 |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1972|11|27}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:This program looks at hormonal changes during puberty, and the socially originated attitudes leading to differences between the sexes.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=mPJUAAAAIBAJ&pg=853%2C5732655 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=November 27, 1972 |page=7 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123651/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=mPJUAAAAIBAJ&pg=853,5732655 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Acupuncture |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1972|12|04}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:An exploration of the traditional art of healing as practiced in China.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98054655/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=December 3, 1972 |page=12-D |access-date=July 4, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818055545/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98054655/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Polar Bear|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|01|08}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The first in a four-part series on Canadian wildlife looks at the polar bear. A pictorial "life history" seen through the seasons; also, the problems of Churchill, Manitoba, a booming town right in the path of a polar bear migration route.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98336304/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 7, 1973 |page=11-D |access-date=July 5, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818040331/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98336304/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Grouse Country|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|01|15}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Mating rituals of the [[Rock ptarmigan|rock]] and [[willow ptarmigan]]; the blue, ruffed and spruce grouse and the [[greater prairie chicken]]; from the offshore islands of Newfoundland to the Alberta foothills.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98336972/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 14, 1973 |page=11-D |access-date=July 8, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818220032/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98336972/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Lobsters and the Sea |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1973|01|22}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A fascinating glimpse into the world of the lobster; an unusual, amusing inhabitant of our oceans and seas.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45947563/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=January 20, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 11, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818011842/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45947563/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Ice Lovers|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|01|29}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A life history of the harp seal, examining the behavior and physiology of this unique Arctic mammal; plus a look at its unusual 800-mile migration each year from Hudson Strait to the Gulf of St. Lawrence.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45950248/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=January 27, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 12, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818194329/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45950248/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Blue Holes of Andros |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|02|13}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:An underwater tour of the mysterious world of the Andros Islands, in the Bahamas, where immense underwater caves have recently been discovered.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46467695/ |title=TV Programs and Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=February 13, 1973 |page=22 |access-date=July 17, 2016 |archive-date=August 19, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160819033849/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46467695/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

<!-- Cities for People was apparently rescheduled from 02/19/1973 to 03/19/1973
{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Cities for People |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|02|19}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:Included are pedestrian malls in Toronto and Stockholm, pioneer and current plans in Germany, car-free zone in Sweden, and new, enlightened thinking in multi-purpose subway systems.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98057580/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=February 18, 1973 |page=10-D}}</ref>}}
-->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Think Before You Eat |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1973|03|05}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:A look at the eating habits of Canadians; food and nutrition, the so-called "Well-balanced diet" and problems of overeating are analyzed in this examination of the dangers of abundance.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98297200/ |title=TV Channels |work=Detroit Free Press |date=March 4, 1973 |page=12-D |access-date=July 22, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818121404/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98297200/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Stockholm '72: Politics For Survival |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1973|03|12}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:A retrospective look at the summer 1972 [[United Nations Conference on the Human Environment|World Conference on the Human Environment]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46149839/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=March 10, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 22, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818094141/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46149839/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Cities for People|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|03|19}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:''The Nature of Things'' presents ''Cities Are For People'', originally scheduled for February 19. It deals with new thinking in urban planning to make cities more livable.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=i_dUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1137%2C4108840 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=March 19, 1973 |page=7 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123652/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=i_dUAAAAIBAJ&pg=1137,4108840 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Migration: Animals in Cycle |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1973|03|26}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:A look at the migratory habits of birds and animals, with recent findings in animal studies reinforced with fascinating film footage of many species in their natural habitats.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46161618/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=March 24, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 24, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818114056/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46161618/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Old Enough|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|04|02}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:This half-hour film depicts obvious absurdities, in a subjective interpretation of [[The Limits to Growth]], a 1970 MIT computer study forecasting economic, social and political collapse by no later than 2020.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46169418/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=March 31, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 24, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818024145/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/46169418/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Recycling: The Garbage Ouroboros|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|04|09}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:A comprehensive examination of the form of pollution fast becoming public enemy number one in North America: garbage.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45753804/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=April 7, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 24, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818192424/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45753804/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Acupuncture|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1973|04|16}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:This ancient and traditional art of healing has been widely practised in China for more than 5,000 years. Its recent rebirth as a successful treatment for many diseases, notably asthma, ulcers, hepatitis, depression, and some forms of deafness and paraplegia, are explored in this program.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45763001/ |title=TV Programs and Highlights |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=April 16, 1973 |page=30 |access-date=July 25, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818004234/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45763001/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Vanishing Peoples: Yanomami |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1973|05|07}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A documentary on the Yanomami of South America, known as The Fierce People, a fast-vanishing tribe in the isolated, tropical rain-forest of the Upper Orinoco River in Venezuela and Brazil.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45911332/ |title=TV Journal |work=The Ottawa Journal |date=May 5, 1973 |page=20 |access-date=July 25, 2016 |archive-date=August 18, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160818010202/https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/45911332/ |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

