== Season 20: 1979–1980 ==
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Flying Circus of Physics / Immune System / Monarch Butterfly / Contact Lenses |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1979|10|24}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premiere: Dr. David Suzuki visits an unusual professor who conducts a [[The Flying Circus of Physics|Flying Circus of Physics]], reports on the latest immunological efforts to treat severely afflicted children, looks at the life cycle of the monarch butterfly and the many hazards it faces, and examines soft contact lenses made for extended periods of use.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=TYlVAAAAIBAJ&pg=1063%2C1480776 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=October 19, 1979 |page=9, Television section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123657/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=TYlVAAAAIBAJ&pg=1063,1480776 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BAtlAAAAIBAJ&pg=1073%2C1584976 |title=Television listings |work=The Edmonton Journal |date=October 24, 1979 |page=D16 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123723/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=BAtlAAAAIBAJ&pg=1073,1584976 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Hypnosis / India's Sacred Cows / Ultra Sound Scanner |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|10|31}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:Dr. David Suzuki reports on the use of hypnosis as a medical application and the use of ultra-sound waves for X-rays, and presents the film "Sacred Cows," dealing with the importance of domestic cattle to the Indian economy.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=c3VkAAAAIBAJ&pg=6037%2C3296218 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=October 26, 1979 |page=48 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123701/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=c3VkAAAAIBAJ&pg=6037,3296218 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Madagascar: Island of the Moon |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1979|11|07}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:The island geographically separated from the rest of the African continent and populated by numerous species of plant and animal life is examined.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=voY1AAAAIBAJ&pg=3234%2C1834847 |title=TV Times |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=November 3, 1979 |page=35 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123645/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=voY1AAAAIBAJ&pg=3234,1834847 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Puppets / One, Two, Three, Zero / New Wave Babies|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|11|14}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:In ''Puppets'', Elizabeth Crocker at the [[IWK Health Centre|Izaak Walton Killam Hospital for Children]], Halifax, is interviewed about her use of puppets to ease children into their hospital stay. The ''One, Two, Three, Zero'' segment examines possible methods of treatment for infertile couples who wish to have children.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://collections.acmi.net.au/objects/33350 |title=One, two, three – zero |work=Australian Centre for the Moving Image |access-date=2016-12-19 |archive-date=2016-12-20 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161220171906/http://collections.acmi.net.au/objects/33350 |url-status=live }}</ref> In ''New Wave Babies'', scientists [[Patrick Steptoe|Dr. Patrick Steptoe]] and Dr. David Bevie are interviewed about methods used in the "test tube baby" procedure.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xIY1AAAAIBAJ&pg=3001%2C5254528 |title=TV Times |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=November 10, 1979 |page=33 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123646/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xIY1AAAAIBAJ&pg=3001,5254528 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Contact|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|11|21}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:The techniques for helping autistic children developed by Barry and Suzi Kaufman to aid their own son are examined, as are their applications in a special program being conducted by a York University graduate.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=l3VkAAAAIBAJ&pg=4439%2C3742750 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=November 16, 1979 |page=47 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123656/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=l3VkAAAAIBAJ&pg=4439,3742750 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Deep Diving / Memory: Come to Think of It|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|11|28}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:David Suzuki goes deep-sea diving to demonstrate the psychological changes divers experience. David also investigates the mysteries of memory – how it is acquired, and how it is lost.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HiNlAAAAIBAJ&pg=4998%2C2201488 |title=James Adams eyes the week |work=The Edmonton Journal |date=November 23, 1979 |page=22, TV Times |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123658/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HiNlAAAAIBAJ&pg=4998,2201488 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://trove.nla.gov.au/work/34944735?q&versionId=43368718 |title=Memory: Come to Think of It (The Nature of Things) |work=National Library of Australia |access-date=2016-12-20 |archive-date=2023-10-23 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023160018/https://trove.nla.gov.au/work/34944735?q&versionId=43368718 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Arctic Oil|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|12|05}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:North America's last great wilderness area, Lancaster Sound in Canada's high Arctic, is now the site of oil exploration, and a unique environment rich in animal and plant life is threatened. David Suzuki and a film crew from The Nature of Things visited Lancaster Sound to find out what the future holds for this region. The material gathered from that expedition, together with interviews with several of the native peoples and with others directly concerned with what is happening in the Canadian Arctic, are presented in this program.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xotVAAAAIBAJ&pg=2020%2C5003633 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=November 30, 1979 |page=9, Television section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123733/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=xotVAAAAIBAJ&pg=2020,5003633 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Clinical Trials / Folk Medicine / Magnetic Bacteria|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|12|12}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:Clinical trials, folk medicine and magnetic bacteria are discussed by Dr. David Suzuki.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HKMyAAAAIBAJ&pg=7029%2C4338068 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=December 12, 1979 |page=46 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123652/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=HKMyAAAAIBAJ&pg=7029,4338068 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Left Brain, Right Brain|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|12|19}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:The latest techniques used in the field of neurosciences are researched to determine the functions of both halves of the human brain.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=CyNlAAAAIBAJ&pg=942%2C4760912 |title=TV listings |work=Edmonton Journal |date=December 19, 1979 |page=B19 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123706/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=CyNlAAAAIBAJ&pg=942,4760912 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Insects / Oyster Culture / Science Fairs |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1979|12|26}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:The survival of insects over thousands of years, the cultivation of oysters in West Africa, and Canadian science fairs on the provincial and national levels are examined by Dr. David Suzuki.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=VvhkAAAAIBAJ&pg=1028%2C2104379 |title=TV listings |work=Edmonton Journal |date=December 26, 1979 |page=E5 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123701/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=VvhkAAAAIBAJ&pg=1028,2104379 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}
