== Season 21: 1980–1981 ==
*Wednesdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Volcanoes / Hovercraft / Curve Balls / Windshear / Xerography |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1980|10|08}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premiere: ''Volcanoes'': A look at the geological formation and environmental impact of volcanoes, focusing on the 1980 eruptions of Mt. St. Helens in Washington. ''Hovercraft'': New uses for air-cushioned vehicles, including the possible future use of hovercraft over muskeg terrain. ''Curve Balls'': A demonstration of how baseball pitchers throw curve balls, and the effects which a baseball has when it spins through the air. ''Windshear'': Richard Malcolm, a physicist specializing in aircraft safety, discusses the problems created by different types of turbulence and the recent aircraft design changes to ameliorate the problems. ''Xerography'': A presentation of how a photocopier works.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=yqYyAAAAIBAJ&pg=1138%2C5082876 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=October 8, 1980 |page=40 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123656/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=yqYyAAAAIBAJ&pg=1138,5082876 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Cystic Fibrosis / Whooping Crane / Cold-Water Survival|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|10|15}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:A report on cystic fibrosis, a genetic respiratory ailment that afflicts young people; the International Crane Foundation's work to save endangered species of birds is documented; and hypothermia, which involves the impact of very cold water on the human body, is examined.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=94FVAAAAIBAJ&pg=5750%2C3604008 |title=Television |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=October 10, 1980 |page=12 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123725/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=94FVAAAAIBAJ&pg=5750,3604008 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Skin – The Bare Necessity / Manatees / Ludhiana |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|10|22}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:Three films are featured: ''Skin – The Bare Necessity'', dealing with the human skin; ''Manatees'', concerning a unique vegetarian creature which lives in coastal waters, and ''[[Ludhiana]]'', a profile of an Indian city which serves as the home base for 12,000 different business enterprises.<ref name="heathercook"/><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=1VsxAAAAIBAJ&pg=3125%2C4321196 |title=TV Times |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=October 18, 1980 |pages=34–35, TV Times |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123711/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=1VsxAAAAIBAJ&pg=3125,4321196 |url-status=live }}</ref>	}}
<!-- second reference provides full title for "Skin – The Bare Necessity" -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tar Wars|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|10|29}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:The geology and relative impact of the [[Athabasca oil sands|Athabasca Tar Sands]] are examined, along with the problems involved in the extraction of oil from the deeper deposits.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=b49VAAAAIBAJ&pg=6967%2C3284114 |title=Television |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=October 24, 1980 |page=12 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123653/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=b49VAAAAIBAJ&pg=6967,3284114 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="TarWars">{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=b49VAAAAIBAJ&pg=1188%2C3273122 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=October 24, 1980 |page=9, Television section |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123739/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=b49VAAAAIBAJ&pg=1188,3273122 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Going Without Gas / Surface Tension / Science Fair|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|11|05}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:''Going Without Gas'': A look at recent developments in designing cars that run on fuels other than petroleum, such as methanol, ethanol, electricity, hydrocarbons and hydrogen. ''Surface Tension'': Observing the molecular properties of surface tension and how soap uses these properties to remove grease. ''Science Fair'': A look at the 1979 Metropolitan Toronto Science Fair, featuring the contestants (ages 12-18), the displays, the judging and the awards presentation.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=n64yAAAAIBAJ&pg=6844%2C3239759 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 5, 1980 |page=92 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123659/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=n64yAAAAIBAJ&pg=6844,3239759 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=China Now – Its Roots in the Past (Part 1 of 2) |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|11|12}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:The lifestyle and culture of modern China are highlighted in a study of the educational system and medical techniques of the country; visits to the seaport of [[Qingdao]] and the ancient cities of [[Hangzhou]] and [[Yangzhou]] are featured.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=qDkyAAAAIBAJ&pg=5021%2C906338 |title=Television listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=November 12, 1980 |page=106 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123703/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=qDkyAAAAIBAJ&pg=5021,906338 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=China Now – The Four Modernizations (Part 2 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|11|19}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:The professional and recreational activities of the Chinese people are examined in a tour of their homes, factories and parks, as well as the palaces which were formerly the homes of emperors.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=rjkyAAAAIBAJ&pg=2962%2C5026068 |title=Television listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=November 19, 1980 |page=106 |access-date=October 5, 2020 |archive-date=October 23, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231023123651/https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=rjkyAAAAIBAJ&pg=2962,5026068 |url-status=live }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Success Story / Sickle Cell Anemia |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|11|26}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:A magazine edition: ''Success Story'': a film by nature photographer John Carey about the insect world and the ability of insects to survive hostile forces of man and nature. ''Sickle Cell Anemia'': A look at what is known about the causes of sickle cell anemia, how it affects its victims and what progress is being made to alleviate and cure the condition.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=g9AyAAAAIBAJ&pg=6726%2C2977051 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 26, 1980 |page=64}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Freak Waves / Invisible Astronomy / Hildebrand|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|12|03}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:''Freak Waves'': A report on work at the Canadian National Research Council into wave formations that can engulf oil rigs or large vessels, even when weather conditions are relatively fair. ''Invisible Astronomy'': Host David Suzuki visits the [[Algonquin Radio Observatory|Algonquin Park Observatory]] and the [[Arecibo Observatory]] in Puerto Rico as he reports on the use of radio astronomy and the search for extraterrestrial intelligent life. ''Hildebrand'': A visit with chemist and educator [[Joel Henry Hildebrand|Joel Hildebrand]] of the University of California.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=zWUxAAAAIBAJ&pg=996%2C1134433 |title=Highlights |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=December 3, 1980 |page=98}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98536942/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=December 7, 1980 |page=28}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Prenatal Diagnosis / Hearing in Chinchillas|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|12|10}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:''Prenatal Diagnosis'': Presents new techniques to examine the fetus while still in the womb to determine its sex and detect possible defects. ''Hearing in Chinchillas'': A look at the similarities between the human ear and the ear of a chinchilla, and a study of how noise can permanently damage hearing in both. Chinchillas are subjected to loud tones and their inner ear membranes are examined for damage in an electron microscope.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=9KIyAAAAIBAJ&pg=6928%2C473681 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=December 10, 1980 |page=58}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Hypnosis / India's Sacred Cows / Ultra Sound Scanner|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1980|12|31}} |ProdCode=repeat}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=AAAS Report / One of the Family / Oyster Culture |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|01|07}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:''AAAS Report'': Scientists gathering in Toronto for a meeting of the American Association for the Advancement of Science are interviewed about the role of the scientific community in the nuclear arms race. ''One of the Family'': A Toronto family whose youngest member was born with cerebral palsy is profiled, focusing on their reactions to his birth and his development with the help of doctors and therapists. ''Oyster Culture'': A look at a Japanese oyster harvesting operation and at oyster farms in Sierra Leone, West Africa, and Sabah, Malaysia.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=DSNlAAAAIBAJ&pg=5637%2C732656 |title=TV Times |work=Edmonton Journal |date=January 2, 1981 |pages=36–37}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Birds' Eggs / Newfoundland Oil / John Polanyi|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|01|14}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:''Birds' Eggs'': A look at the many ways that egg shells protect and aid embryonic development of birds. ''Newfoundland Oil'': The technical difficulties and possible environmental and social impact of oil drilling off the coast of Newfoundland are examined. ''[[John Polanyi]]'': An interview with the [[University of Toronto]] molecular scientist who discusses his work, his influences, the responsibilities of a scientist and the centrality of science in the modern world.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=6XNkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5545%2C4948692 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=January 9, 1981 |page=43}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Poisoned Playgrounds / Charlie|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|01|28}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:The actions taken by Ontario parents when insecticide spraying at a school proved hazardous to their children's health are examined, and in a repeat showing of the film "Charlie", the work of Canadian paleontologist Charlie Sternberg is highlighted.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Ze4yAAAAIBAJ&pg=6814%2C2165245 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=January 28, 1981 |page=58}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=High Altitude Physiology / The Vision of Galileo / Dust Storms|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|02|04}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The effects of high altitudes on man's physiology, the vision of Galileo, and the causes and results of dust storms are examined.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jfhkAAAAIBAJ&pg=3718%2C6192812 |title=TV Times |work=Edmonton Journal |date=January 30, 1981 |page=34}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Moving Still|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|02|11}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:A look at the history of scientific photography from its beginnings in 1837 to the present-day use of high-speed cameras.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=XKJlAAAAIBAJ&pg=4827%2C165139 |title=TV Highlights |work=The Vancouver Sun |date=February 11, 1981 |page=C2}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Sri Lanka: Island of Serendib|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|02|18}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
:A documentary on Sri Lanka, its wildlife and culture and the changes taking place as technological development erodes traditional ways of life.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=yKFVAAAAIBAJ&pg=1345%2C3559440 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=February 13, 1981 |page=B9}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Seal Psychology / Chiricahuas / Violins|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|02|25}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Seal psychology, the Chiricahua mountains, and the making of violins are among the topics covered.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=n3RkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5656%2C6813740 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=February 20, 1981 |page=46, TV Times}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tar Wars|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|03|04}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The geology and relative impact of the Athabasca Tar Sands are examined, along with the problems involved in the extraction of oil from the deeper deposits.<ref name="TarWars" /><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=JpJVAAAAIBAJ&pg=4007%2C3801354 |title=Television |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=February 27, 1981 |page=F12}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Blackfly / Desalination / Memory – Come to think of it / Dr. Karl Illmensee|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|03|11}} |ProdCode=16 |ShortSummary=
:''Blackfly'': A look at the life cycle of the blackfly, and its effects in northern Canada and Africa. ''Desalination'': A practical method of desalination using reverse osmosis is presented. ''Memory – Come to think of it'' (repeat): A look at recent research into the brain's memory capacities. ''Dr. Karl Illmensee'': A look at the work being done by {{Interlanguage link|Karl Illmensee|lt=Dr. Karl Illmensee|de}} at the University of Geneva to study the possibility of causing cancerous cells to revert to normal cells.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=Nmk1AAAAIBAJ&pg=1274%2C361733 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=March 11, 1981 |page=66}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Foxes' Earth|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|03|25}} |ProdCode=17 |ShortSummary=
:For centuries the people of the village of Huasicancha in Peru lived under the domination of others, from the last of the Inca rulers to the Spanish conquerors and subsequent regimes. How the people finally rose up to reclaim the poor land they farmed at a subsistence level is told in this documentary.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Peacock |first=Bruce |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=gJhVAAAAIBAJ&pg=1176%2C1535390 |title=Tube Talk |work=The Leader-Post |location=Regina, Saskatchewan |date=March 20, 1981 |page=B9}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b6e551379 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170103100338/http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b6e551379 |url-status=dead |archive-date=January 3, 2017 |title=The Foxes' Earth |work=British Film Institute |access-date=2017-01-02}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Last of Life / The Cajuns|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|04|01}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:''The Last of Life'': A look at geriatric medicine and some aspects of research into the biology of aging. ''The Cajuns'': The descendants of Nova Scotia's Acadians and their lifestyle are profiled at their adopted home, the Bayou Lafourche in southern Louisiana.}}

|}

== Season 22: 1981–1982 ==
*Wednesdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Reconnective Surgery / Shark Vision / Reclaiming the Desert / The Battery|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|10|14}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premiere: David Suzuki examines reconnective surgery and the vision capabilities of sharks, visits an irrigation project in India and explains the functions of batteries. The micro-surgery segment was taped in China and features Dr. Chen Chung Wei of the 6th People's Hospital in Shanghai. Dr. Chen is credited with pioneering the techniques now being practiced at Toronto's General hospital.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Knowler |first=Brad |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=XndkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5689%2C6155031 |title=Worth Watching |work=The Calgary Herald |date=October 9, 1981 |pages=50–51, TV Times section}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Rabies / Island of Coral / Microwave Ovens|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|10|21}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:Steps being taken to combat rabies in Ontario, an ''Island of Coral'' which provides a home for some of the world's most unique creatures, and the advantages and hazards of microwave ovens.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=wjsyAAAAIBAJ&pg=3099%2C396166 |title=Television listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=October 21, 1981}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Edge of the Cold (Part 1 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|10|28}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:[[David Parer]]'s Australian examination of the wildlife on [[Macquarie Island]], narrated by [[Edmund Hillary|Sir Edmund Hillary]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=NXdkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5797%2C2332094 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=October 23, 1981}}</ref> The Australian Broadcasting Corporation produced four half-hour films about the Macquarie Islands. This episode of The Nature of Things compiles two of them. The other two episodes are broadcast in "Edge of the Cold part 2" on 8 December 1982.}}
<!-- Reference for "Edge Of The Cold" title: -->
<!-- https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=yAowAAAAIBAJ&sjid=CKUFAAAAIBAJ&pg=2771%2C2278983 -->
<!-- date of broadcast for this reference is different, but the description is the same -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Waterproof Frog / The Piano / In the Sub-Nuclear Kitchen / The Record|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|11|04}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:''Waterproof Frog'': A look at the unique frog ''[[Phyllomedusa]]'' which lives in the arid [[Gran Chaco]] region of central South America. The frog protects itself against water loss by coating its body with a waxy secretion. ''The Piano'': A program about the history and science of the piano. ''In the Sub-Nuclear Kitchen'': A brief look at particle physics and the huge particle accelerator at the [[CERN|European Organization for Nuclear Research]] (CERN) in Geneva. ''The Record'': A history of the phonograph record.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=QY0kAAAAIBAJ&pg=2813%2C1065798 |title=Television Listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=November 4, 1981 |page=84}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Magic Way of Going|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|11|11}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:A study of the genetic and biological development of thoroughbred horses, tracing the history of the animal from the time that horses were small creatures to the sleek, larger size animals they are today.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99805099/ |title=Television Listings |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 11, 1981 |page=11C}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Twins – And Then There Were Two|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|11|18}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:This is a special edition based on two half hour programs that first aired in 1978. Producer Heather Cook has revised and updated the original programs to include the latest research data in studies that have been continuing over long periods at various medical centres and universities in North America. The program explains how cells divide to produce twins and the differences between fraternal twins and identical twins.<ref name="heathercook"/><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99811023/ |title=Television |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 18, 1981 |page=12D}}</ref>}}
<!-- Second reference provides the complete program title -->

<!-- "Tipping The Scales" was rescheduled from 12/02/1981 to 01/13/1982, so commenting out this entry
{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tipping The Scales|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|12|02}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:This program focuses on current research being done on the problems of obesity and anorexia nervosa, the role diet plays in weight control and the genetic predisposition towards obesity.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Morrow |first=Martin |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=pHVkAAAAIBAJ&sjid=g34NAAAAIBAJ&pg=2241%2C896212 |title=Tonight |work=The Calgary Herald |date=December 2, 1981 |page=B22}}</ref>}}
 -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Natural Turn of Events / Kidney Transplant / Kelp|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1981|12|09}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:''A Natural Turn of Events'': Construction in Toronto has led to the creation of a long spit of land – the Leslie Street Spit – which is turning into a prime nesting location for many kinds of birds, and also for migrating [[monarch butterflies]]. ''Kidney Transplant'': Kidney dialysis and transplants, with Dr. Michael Robinette of the Toronto General Hospital. ''Kelp'': A look at kelp harvesting in China, and the products that can be made from it.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=qnVkAAAAIBAJ&pg=1122%2C5966227 |title=Tonight on Television |work=The Calgary Herald |date=December 9, 1981 |page=B14}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Microscope: Making It Big / Desert Doctors / Polar Bear Pass|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|01|06}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:''Microscope: Making It Big'': A look at the history and present development of the microscope. ''Desert Doctors'': A look at the mobile hospitals used to treat people inexpensively in India's [[Thar Desert|Rajisthan Desert]]. ''Polar Bear Pass'': A look at [[Polar Bear Pass]], an important oasis of arctic wildlife on Canada's [[Bathurst Island (Nunavut)|Bathurst Island]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99795734/ |title=Television |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 6, 1982 |page=8C}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tipping The Scales|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|01|13}} |ProdCode=09|ShortSummary=
:The various factors which influence human weight are examined through reports on eating habits, diets, and basic metabolism.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=1v0hAAAAIBAJ&pg=4039%2C734572 |title=Television listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=January 13, 1982}}</ref> Rescheduled from 12/02/1981.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99798247/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 10, 1982 |page=30}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=An Island Shall a Monster Make / Philip Morrison on Nuclear War|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|01|20}} |ProdCode=10|ShortSummary=
:A magazine edition highlights the giant lizards on the island of [[Isla de Mona|Mona]] near Puerto Rico, and physics professor [[Philip Morrison]]'s work on the atomic bomb as a team member on the [[Manhattan Project]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=83ZkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5877%2C3093404 |title=TV Times |work=The Calgary Herald |date=January 15, 1982 |page=44, TV Times}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b72f4f4d2 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170113095425/http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b72f4f4d2 |url-status=dead |archive-date=January 13, 2017 |title=An Island Shall a Monster Make |work=British Film Institute |access-date=2017-01-10}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Aspirin / Windy Bay / Fluorescent Light|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|01|27}} |ProdCode=11|ShortSummary=
:''Aspirin'': An examination of how the common drug aspirin may have widespread application in combating heart and circulatory system diseases. This depends on its action, only recently appreciated, of inhibiting blood clotting. ''Windy Bay'': A look at Windy Bay, on [[Lyell Island]] in the Queen Charlotte Islands, one of the last areas of virgin rainforest on the Canadian west coast. Windy Bay is now threatened by clearcut logging even though it has been suggested as a priority site for international conservation. ''Fluorescent Light'': How fluorescent lights work and how they are manufactured.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=B_4hAAAAIBAJ&pg=3065%2C2630745 |title=Television listings |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=January 27, 1982 |page=72}}</ref>}}
<!-- "Tide Mill" was apparently rescheduled from 01/27/1982 to 03/10/1982; replaced with "Fluorescent Light" -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Hanuman Langurs: Monkeys of India / A Helping Hand / Formation Flight|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|02|03}} |ProdCode=12|ShortSummary=
:''Hanuman Langurs: Monkeys of India'': A look at the social organization and adaptation to human settlements of [[Gray langur|Hanuman langurs]], social monkeys who are named for the monkey god Hanuman. ''A Helping Hand'': A look at myoelectric prostheses, artificial limbs which, while being powered by batteries, are actually controlled by amplified muscle electricity. ''Formation Flight'': Examining the reasons why large birds tend to fly in formation.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/100223095/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 31, 1982 |page=38}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Jute Plastic / The Life of the Honeybee / Dr. Joel Hildebrand|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|02|24}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:''Jute Plastic'': The harvesting and processing of Bangladesh's [[jute]] fibre. Jute sheets coated with polyester resin and laminated together form a strong cheap material which may win an international market for jute. ''The Life of the Honeybee'': A documentary following the life cycle of the honey bee and the social organization of the hive. ''Dr. Joel Hildebrand'': David Suzuki travels to the University of California at Berkeley to attend the celebration of the 100th birthday of [[Joel Hildebrand|Dr. Joel Hildebrand]], who is active in science after teaching chemistry for many years.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=x2UxAAAAIBAJ&pg=3422%2C943537 |title=Television Today |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=February 24, 1982 |page=G2}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Mind's Eye / Tide Mill / Colour It Snake|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|03|10}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:''Mind's Eye'': A report on recent research into how the brain constructs vision from the information supplied by the eyes. ''Tide Mill'': A look at a grist mill at Ealing in England which for two centuries has run on tidal power. ''Colour It Snake'': A discussion of the ways in which the basic pigments in snake scales are arranged to produce colour patterns fitting various survival needs.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=5WUxAAAAIBAJ&pg=2870%2C4092691 |title=Television Today |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=March 10, 1982 |page=F2}}</ref>}}
<!-- 03/17/1982: "Going Without Gas / Surface Tension / Science Fair" (repeat from 11/05/1980) -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Asteroid and the Dinosaur|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|03|24}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
:An examination of the theory advanced by physicist [[Luis Walter Alvarez|Luis Alvarez]] and others that an asteroid impact was responsible for the sudden total extinction of the dinosaurs over sixty million years ago.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=FaMyAAAAIBAJ&pg=1393%2C1310488 |title=Television |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=March 24, 1982 |page=40}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Waves / The Harp Seal / Blackfly|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|03|31}} |ProdCode=16 |ShortSummary=
:''Waves'': An update of the program "Freak Waves" originally broadcast in December 1980. A further look at research being done into wave formations that can destroy oil rigs on the open sea. ''The Harp Seal'' (repeat): The development of the harp seal is traced, from birth through nursing to its eventual migration northward. ''Blackfly'' (repeat): A look at disease and other problems caused by blackflies.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/97814001/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=March 28, 1982 |page=44}}</ref>}}

|}

== Season 23: 1982–1983 ==
*Wednesdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Gutenberg Revisited / Diving Birds / Tulips |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|10|20}} |ProdCode= 01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premier: ''Gutenberg Revisited'': A look at new developments in microelectronic information processing, focusing on the [[Telidon]] system, a Canadian invention offering two-way interactive television. ''Diving Birds'': A look at adaptations in aquatic birds such as ducks and geese which allow them to make long dives under water. ''Tulips'': An overview of the tulip industry in Holland.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Boone |first=Mike |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=YywyAAAAIBAJ&pg=4205%2C2499589 |title='The Nature of Things' maintains steady flow of excellence |work=The Montreal Gazette |date=Oct 16, 1982 |pages=4, 33 TV Times}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Northern Games / Geothermal Energy / Ships of the Desert / Coriolis Effect|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|10|27}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:''Northern Games'': A look at the traditional games of the Inuit as they are practised 800&nbsp;km north of the Arctic Circle, by youth in competition from communities across the North.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.nfb.ca/film/northern_games/ |title=Northern Games |work=National Film Board of Canada |access-date=2017-01-18}}</ref> ''Geothermal Energy'': A look at how geothermal energy has been adapted to supply human needs on Iceland. ''Ships of the Desert'': An exploration of the dromedary camel, adapted for life in the desert. ''Coriolis Effect'': A brief explanation of the coriolis effect – what it is, how it is demonstrable, and its effect on weather.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=W9czAAAAIBAJ&pg=3081%2C1689061 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=October 23, 1982 |page=31}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Bring Back My Bonnie|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|11|03}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:A look at recovery after strokes. In previous years, strokes were frequently fatal, and brain damage was seen as permanent. Now, all this is changing. It has been found that with therapy many stroke victims can recover some or even most of the functions they have lost.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/98991684/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=October 31, 1982 |page=40}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Long Point|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|11|10}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:[[Long Point, Ontario|Long point marsh]] is a sandspit on the northern shore of Lake Erie. Discovered in 1670 by French explorers, this wildlife area has kept many of its original features and is now an important habitat for many species of animals and migrating birds.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.wildfilmhistory.org/film/473/Long+Point.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121110210103/http://www.wildfilmhistory.org/film/473/Long+Point.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=November 10, 2012 |title=Nature of Things: Long Point |work=Wildscreen |access-date=2017-01-20}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=zaMyAAAAIBAJ&pg=2406%2C3434457 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 6, 1982 |page=30}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Living in a Sunhouse / Brittle Bones / DIAL|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|11|17}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:Featured: Solar techniques to improve the thermal efficiency of a house; treatment methods for fragile bones ([[osteoporosis]]); and a laser system called [[Atmospheric lidar#Gases|DIAL]] (Differential Absorption Lidar) that measures levels of environmental pollution.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=fr4yAAAAIBAJ&pg=2502%2C1866913 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 13, 1982 |page=29}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Superachievers (Japan part 1 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|11|24}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:The landmarks of Japanese science and technology since the end of World War II are highlighted in the first of two related programs. The ancient craft of Samurai swordmaking and computer based steel production are also examined.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=hL4yAAAAIBAJ&pg=2468%2C5586128 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 20, 1982 |page=30}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Nation Family (Japan part 2 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|12|01}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:The everyday life of Japanese workers is traced through their values, their leisure activities and the mechanization of their factories.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1982-12-01-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 1, 1982 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Edge of the Cold (Part 2 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|12|08}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:The Australian Broadcasting Corporation produced four half-hour films about the Macquarie Islands. This episode of The Nature of Things compiles two of them. The first was originally entitled 'Man the Hunter, Man the Keeper.' The second was originally entitled 'The Dominant Male.' The program looks at the delicate ecological balance which must be maintained for the populations of elephant seals and seabirds on the Macquarie Islands. Narrated by Sir Edmund Hillary.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1982-12-08-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 8, 1982 |page=18}}</ref> The other two episodes were compiled into a Nature of Things broadcast of 28 October 1981.}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Tipping the Scales|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|12|15}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The various factors which influence human weight are examined through reports on eating habits, diets, and basic metabolism.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1982-12-15-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 15, 1982 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=To Be or Not to Be|OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1982|12|22}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:Numerous questions raised by new genetic testing techniques designed to identify fetal disorders are addressed. The evolving techniques of fetal diagnosis such as [[amniocentesis]], [[Ultrasound#Human medicine|ultrasound]] and [[fetoscopy]] are also considered.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b6de62173 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170202071047/http://www.bfi.org.uk/films-tv-people/4ce2b6de62173 |url-status=dead |archive-date=February 2, 2017 |title=The Nature of Things: Prenatal Diagnosis: To Be or Not to Be |work=British Film Institute |access-date=2017-01-23}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1982-12-22-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 22, 1982 |page=18}}</ref> This is a revised version of 'Prenatal Diagnosis' which was originally broadcast on December 10, 1980.}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Fragile Mountain|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1982|12|29}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:An examination of the measures being taken by a Himalayan mountain community that is trying to avoid a flood disaster, such as that which devastated northern India in 1978.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1982-12-29-17 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 29, 1982 |page=17}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Newborn|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|01|12}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A look at the first moments of an infant's life and its adaptation to the outside world.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-01-12-15 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 12, 1983 |page=15}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Decade of Delay / RH Laboratory / Hawaii Telescope|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|01|19}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:''Decade of Delay'': A look at what can be done to make cars safer, and an inquiry into why it is not being done. ''RH Laboratory'': A visit to the special Rh. laboratory in Winnipeg, which was the world pioneer in combating [[Rh disease]], an infant condition that results from the presence or absence of the rhesus factor in individual blood cells. ''Hawaii Telescope'': A look at the telescope and observatory erected by a joint venture of Canada and France on [[Mauna Kea]] volcano in Hawaii.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-01-19-17 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 19, 1983 |page=17}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Water: Friend or Foe? |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|01|26}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:Holland's [[Delta Works|Delta Project]], a task involving difficult measures to reclaim land from the sea, is detailed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-01-26-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 26, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Gentle Giants / Ancient Diseases / Water Weeds|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|02|02}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:''The Gentle Giants'': A film on the [[gray whale]]s who live off the pacific coast of North America. ''Ancient Diseases'': A look at [[paleopathology]] and what can be learned about the past of man and the history of diseases through the autopsy of ancient human remains. ''Water Weeds'': A look at an experimental project in [[Listowel, Ontario|Listowel]], Ontario, using [[Typha|cattails]] to purify sewage. The cattails thrive in sewage where they also filter out some industrial contaminants as well as deal with organic compounds.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://theprogress.newspapers.com/image/77119039/ |title=Wednesday Highlights |work=The Chilliwack Progress |date=February 2, 1983 |page=2D}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Hanuman Langurs: Monkeys of India / A Helping Hand / Formation Flight|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|02|09}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Hanuman Langurs: Monkeys of India: A look at the social organization and adaptation to human settlements of Hanuman langurs, social monkeys who are named for the monkey god Hanuman. A Helping Hand: A look at myoelectric prostheses, artificial limbs which, while being powered by batteries, are actually controlled by amplified muscle electricity. Formation Flight: Examining the reasons why large birds tend to fly in formation.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-02-09-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 9, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Jute Plastic / The Life of the Honeybee / Dr. Joel Hildebrand|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|02|16}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The harvesting and processing of Bangladesh's jute fiber, the life cycle of a honeybee colony, and the work of Berkeley professor Dr. Joel Hildebrand are highlighted.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-02-16-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 16, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=An Island Shall a Monster Make / Philip Morrison on Nuclear War|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|02|23}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A magazine edition highlights the giant lizards on the island of Mona near Puerto Rico, physics professor Philip Morrison's work on the atomic bomb as a team member on the Manhattan Project, and water in its frozen state.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-02-23-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 23, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Magnet Earth|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|03|02}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:A one-hour film from the BBC series Horizon exploring the effects of the Earth's magnetic field on animals and, to a lesser extent, humans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://theprogress.newspapers.com/image/77119343/ |title=Wednesday Highlights |work=The Chilliwack Progress |date=March 2, 1983 |page=2D}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Waterproof Frog / The Piano / In the Sub-Nuclear Kitchen / The Record|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|03|09}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:''Waterproof Frog'': A look at the unique frog ''Phyllomedusa'' which lives in the arid Gran Chaco region of central South America. The frog protects itself against water loss by coating its body with a waxy secretion. ''The Piano'': A program about the history and science of the piano. ''In the Sub-Nuclear Kitchen'': A brief look at particle physics and the huge particle accelerator at the European Centre for Nuclear Research (CERN) in Geneva. ''The Record'': A history of the phonograph record.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://theprogress.newspapers.com/image/77119482/ |title=Wednesday Highlights |work=The Chilliwack Progress |date=March 9, 1983 |page=2D}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Sri Lanka, Temple of the Elephant"; alternate title: "Sri Lanka: Island of Serendib|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|03|16}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The island paradise of Sri Lanka is visited in a comprehensive study that examines the dangers its wildlife faces from cultural and technological changes.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-03-16-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=March 16, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}
<!-- -->
<!-- 03/23/1983: No episode – pre-empted for "Genie Awards" -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=On The Track of the Wild Otter|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|03|30}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
:The social life of one of nature's shyest creatures is examined in a year-round study of its behavior.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://theprogress.newspapers.com/image/77120199/ |title=Wednesday Highlights |work=The Chilliwack Progress |date=March 30, 1983 |page=2D}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Taylor |first=Marsali |url=http://www.shetlandtimes.co.uk/2012/02/07/lights-camera-action-reestit-mutton |title=Lights, camera, action… reestit mutton! |work=The Shetland Times |date=July 2, 2012}}</ref>}}
<!-- Second ref is to show what appears to be the correct title, "On The Track of the Wild Otter" -->

|}

== Season 24: 1983–1984 ==
*Wednesdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Swimming / Diabetes: Beating the Needle / Glass Eyes |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|10|05}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premiere: Dr. David Suzuki profiles veteran Canadian swimmer Dan Thompson, the lifestyles of diabetics and the manufacturing of glass eyes.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-10-05-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 5, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Cobra: India's Good Snake / Blue Babies / High Flight|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|10|12}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:''Cobra: India's Good Snake'': Ignorance and superstition surround the cobra, threatening the members of this species which is helpful to man. ''Blue Babies'': David Suzuki talks with cardiologist Peter Olley of the Hospital for Sick Children in Toronto concerning the pharmaceutical and medical treatment of infants born with a congenital heart defect. ''High Flight'': Research is beginning to uncover the reasons why birds can fly at high altitudes that would cause brain damage in humans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-10-12-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 12, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Animal Imposters|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|10|19}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:The clever methods of various creatures either to hunt or to avoid being hunted are examined in locations including Central America and Australia.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-10-19-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 19, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Spas: Magic or Medicine?|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|10|26}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:England, Japan, France and Toronto are among the locations for a study of treatments at spas for health conditions.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-10-26-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 26, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Cathedral Engineers / Neem: A Natural Insecticide / Bluebird Trails |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|11|02}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:''The Cathedral Engineers'': Shot on location in France and New York City, the program looks at the history and philosophy of European gothic cathedrals. ''Neem: A Natural Insecticide'': Products of the [[Azadirachta indica|neem]], one of the world's most useful trees, are used to make everything from soap to insecticide. ''Bluebird Trails'': Pushed out of prime nesting sites by the introduction of the English sparrow and starling in 1900, the North American [[bluebird]] is making a comeback thanks to specially constructed bluebird boxes built across eastern North America.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-11-02-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=November 2, 1983 |page=18}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/97554485/ |title=the tv page |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 2, 1983 |page=8D}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Salmon on the Line |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|11|09}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:This hour-long program documents the reasons for the decline of some species of pacific salmon. The life cycles, spawning, and migration of various types of pacific salmon are studied.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=CK8yAAAAIBAJ&pg=2626%2C2981024 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 5, 1983 |page=37}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.endangeredspecieshandbook.org/video2_films_wildlifeplant_fish.php |title=Video – Films |work=Endangered Species Handbook |publisher=Animal Welfare Institute |access-date=2017-02-02}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Bishnois and the Antelope / Cyclosporin / Freezing Water |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|11|16}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:''Bishnois and the Antelope'': a Hindu sect known as the [[Bishnoi]]s live on the edge of the Rajasthan desert in northwestern India. Strict vegetarians, they have an awareness of ecology which makes them protectors of their environment. ''Cyclosporin'': A new anti-rejection drug [[Ciclosporin|cyclosporin]] is being used to treat transplant patients. ''Freezing Water'': A look at what happens when water is frozen.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-11-16-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=November 16, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Footsteps on the Moon / Salamanders / Iron Age Village |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|11|23}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:The moon's role in man's history, the anatomy of salamanders, and the discovery of an iron-age village are highlighted.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=lb4yAAAAIBAJ&pg=2331%2C6262387 |title=TV Times |work=The Ottawa Citizen |date=November 19, 1983 |page=35}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Natural Turn of Events / Kidney Transplant / Kelp |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|11|30}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:''A Natural Turn of Events'': Construction in Toronto has led to the creation of a long spit of land – the Leslie Street Spit – which is turning into a prime nesting location for many kinds of birds, and also for migrating monarch butterflies. ''Kidney Transplant'': Kidney dialysis and transplants, with Dr. Michael Robinette of the Toronto General Hospital. ''Kelp'': A look at kelp harvesting in China, and the products that can be made from it.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99186002/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 27, 1983 |page=52}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Snappers / Inside Out / Samurai Armour |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|12|07}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:Featured: the snapping turtle is profiled; a look at technology which enables doctors to examine the interior of the body without surgical intervention, and a visit to a Japanese craftsman's workshop where Samurai armour is made.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-12-07-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 7, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Flight Simulators / Beating The Blues|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|12|21}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:A visit to a Montreal company that produces sophisticated devices to train air pilots, and a report on the effects of severe depression and methods of treatment.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-12-21-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 21, 1983 |page=20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Making it Big / Tulips / Diving Birds|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1983|12|28}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:This episode looks at the invention of the microscope, a report on Holland's tulip industry, and diving birds.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1983-12-28-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 28, 1983 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Maps: From Quill to Computer / Mountain Gophers / Japanese Silk Weaving|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|01|04}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:''Maps: From Quill to Computer'': The history of mapmaking, from early clay tablets to state-of-the-art renditions. ''Mountain Gophers'': a look at the [[Columbian ground squirrel]] found in the area of the Rocky Mountains, their mating and territorial habits and methods of communication with each other. ''Japanese Silk Weaving'': A look at the production of silk, from cocoon to fabric.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99167733/ |title=the tv page |work=Detroit Free Press |date=January 4, 1984 |page=8C}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-01-04-20 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 4, 1984 |page=13}}</ref>}}
<!-- "Erie Ice", mentioned in the refs, was apparently rescheduled to 11/07/1984, -->
<!-- and replaced with "Japanese Silk Weaving" -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=A Magic Way of Going|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|01|11}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The genetic and biological development of thoroughbred horses is traced in a program filmed in Canada, the United Kingdom, Ireland, Maryland and Kentucky.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-01-11-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 11, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Superachievers (Japan part 1 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|01|18}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The landmarks of Japanese science and technology since the end of World War II are highlighted in the first of two related programs. The ancient craft of Samurai swordmaking and computer based steel production are also examined.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-01-18-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 18, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Nation Family (Japan part 2 of 2)|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|01|25}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:The everyday life of Japanese workers is traced through their values, their leisure activities and the mechanization of their factories.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-01-25-16 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 25, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Long Point Marsh|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|02|01}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:An examination of the 32-kilometer-long sandspit on the north shore of Lake Erie, which serves as home base for numerous small creatures and is now under the jurisdiction of the Canadian Wildlife Service.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-02-01-16 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 1, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Bring Back My Bonnie|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|02|08}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:This documentary details the treatment and rehabilitation of stroke victims at Sunnybrook and Riverdale Hospitals, the Toronto Rehabilitation Centre and the homes of several victims struggling to recover.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-02-08-14 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 8, 1984 |page=14}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Decade of Delay / RH Laboratory / Hawaii Telescope|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|02|15}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:''Decade of Delay'': A look at what can be done to make cars safer, and an inquiry into why it is not being done. ''RH Laboratory'': A visit to the special Rh. laboratory in Winnipeg, which was the world pioneer in combating Rh disease, an infant condition that results from the presence or absence of the rhesus factor in individual blood cells. ''Hawaii Telescope'': A look at the telescope and observatory erected by a joint venture of Canada and France on Mauna Kea volcano in Hawaii.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-02-15-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 15, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Gentle Giants / Ancient Diseases / Water Weeds|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|02|22}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:A magazine edition features a West German film about gray whales, a profile of scientists studying million-year-old human remains, and an experiment involving sewage treatment in Listowel, Ontario.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-02-22-17 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 22, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Water: Friend or Foe?|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|02|29}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:Holland's Delta Project, a task involving difficult measures to reclaim land from the sea, is detailed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-02-29-16 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=February 29, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Edge of the Cold|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|03|07}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:David Parer's Australian examination of the wildlife on Macquarie Island, narrated by Sir Edmund Hillary.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-03-07-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=March 7, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

|}

== Season 25: 1984–1985 ==
*Wednesdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Pain in the Back / Mobile Computers / The Birdmappers: Bird Atlas|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|10|10}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:Season Premiere: Treating chronic back pain; mobile data terminals for emergency personnel; and individuals tracking birds in Ontario and observing their behaviour so that a bird atlas can be published.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-10-10-16 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 10, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Kunde Hospital / Computer Choreology / Periscope Camera|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|10|17}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:A visit to [[Kunde Hospital]], built in a remote Himalayan village by New Zealand explorer Sir Edmund Hillary; a look at the use of computers for dance notation which record a dancer's movement; and a camera that can perform complex film tracking shots through miniature sets.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-10-17-16 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 17, 1984 |page=16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Prairie Waters|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|10|24}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:An exploration of Manitoba's Delta Marsh and its animal, bird and plant life.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99117377/ |title=the tv page |work=Detroit Free Press |date=October 24, 1984 |page=7D}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Miracle of Life|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|10|31}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:Host David Suzuki presents film footage of the reproductive process of [[mitosis]], the first division of an egg that initiates the process of reproducing life. The story follows the development of the human fetus from conception until it enters the outside world.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99122164/ |title=the tv page |work=Detroit Free Press |date=October 31, 1984 |page=6D}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-10-31-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=October 31, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Chinese Wall Paintings / Erie Ice / Fly Fishing|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|11|07}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:''Chinese Wall Paintings'': Observing the detailed and time-consuming work involved in restoring two large 14th-century wall paintings owned by the [[Royal Ontario Museum]]. ''Erie Ice'': A look at the formation of ice ridges that can force themselves down into the lake bottom, carving huge gouges when they shift. ''Fly Fishing'': A look at the sport of fly fishing, examining the life cycle of the brook trout and the mayfly and showing how detailed knowledge of the river ecosystem is necessary for successful angling.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99131593/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 4, 1984 |page=56}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Sexual Encounters of the Floral Kind |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|11|14}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:A look at how various species of plant life lure insects and animals to effect the pollination process.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.wildfilmhistory.org/film/121/Sexual+Encounters+of+the+Floral+Kind.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081123043734/http://www.wildfilmhistory.org/film/121/Sexual+Encounters+of+the+Floral+Kind.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=November 23, 2008 |title=Sexual Encounters of the Floral Kind |work=Wildscreen |access-date=2017-02-13}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-11-14-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=November 14, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Drought in Africa / Where the Bay Becomes the Sea / Insect Communication|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|11|28}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:''Drought in Africa'': A brief look at the drought conditions in Ethiopia and the need for solutions at the village level. ''Where the Bay Becomes the Sea'': A documentary about the fragile and complex marine ecosystem in the Bay of Fundy. The film traces relationships within the food chain – from tiny plankton to birds and seals and finally to whales and humans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://www.nfb.ca/film/where_the_bay_becomes_the_sea/ |title=Where the Bay Becomes the Sea |work=National Film Board of Canada |access-date=2017-02-14}}</ref> ''Insect Communication'': A look at the hearing and sound-producing mechanisms of insects, used for attracting a mate, defining territory, and defending against bats.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99178132/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=November 25, 1984 |page=56}}</ref>}}
<!-- ref for same episode, later date, lists drought segment: https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/159557886/ -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Making Moves / Orchids / Juggling|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|12|12}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:''Making Moves'': Research into the technological advances that allow victims of spinal cord injuries to regain motor skills.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-12-12-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 12, 1984 |page=18}}</ref> ''Orchids'': A look at some of the varieties of wild orchids and Canadian orchids, and interviews with orchid experts. ''Juggling'': Examining the science of juggling, including some impressive demonstrations of juggling.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99178403/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=December 9, 1984 |page=50}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Salmon on the Line|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1984|12|19}} |ProdCode=repeat |ShortSummary=
:David Suzuki takes a look at how the once-plentiful Pacific salmon is now threatened with extinction.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/99154899/ |title=The TV Book |work=Detroit Free Press |date=December 16, 1984 |page=48}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1984-12-19-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=December 19, 1984 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Voices in the Wind |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|1984|12|26}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:A historical look at the evolution of the pipe organ.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=jHZkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5904%2C522860 |title=TV Times |work=Calgary Herald |date=December 21, 1984 |page=42}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Dinosaurs: Remains to be Seen|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1985|01|09}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:The program looks at the work of various experts in the fields of paleontology, geology, biology, and art as they study the evidence and history of dinosaurs and their environment.<ref>{{cite book |title=Dinosaurs, remains to be seen |isbn=9788126517824 |oclc=42484618 |last1=Walker |first1=Jearl |date=30 July 2008 |publisher=Wiley India Pvt. Limited }}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1985-01-09-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 9, 1985 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=CPR: Reversing Sudden Death / Catching the Wind / Environmental Sculpture|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1985|01|16}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:''CPR: Reversing Sudden Death'': a British Columbia campaign to teach [[cardiopulmonary resuscitation]] to the general population. ''Catching the Wind'': An examination of the skill and scientific expertise required for world-class sailing. ''Environmental Sculpture'': In his studio in Oakville, sculptor Joseph Patriska creates art which urges government and industry to commit to a cleaner environment.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1985-01-16-18 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 16, 1985 |page=18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Lakes: Troubled Waters|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1985|01|23}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:David Suzuki takes a look at the potential hazards of pollution and mismanagement of one of the world's greatest sources of fresh water – the Great Lakes.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=wXZkAAAAIBAJ&pg=5720%2C3865949 |title=TV Times |work=Calgary Herald |date=January 18, 1985 |page=42}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=''A Planet for the Taking'' Overview / Blue Babies / High Flight|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|1985|01|30}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:Preview of the new series, ''A Planet for the Taking''; pharmaceutical and medical treatment of infants born with a congenital heart defect; and the anatomy of the bird's lung and the biochemistry of its brain that makes high flight possible.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=http://pgnewspapers.pgpl.ca/fedora/repository/pgc:1985-01-30-14 |title=TV Schedule |work=The Citizen |location=Prince George, British Columbia |date=January 30, 1985 |page=14}}</ref><ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|url=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/173517205/ |title=Television Times |work=Los Angeles Times |date=March 18, 1984 |page=Thirty}}</ref>}}

|}

