{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Bhopal: The Search for Justice|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2004|12|09}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:''Bhopal: The Search for Justice'' looks at the [[Bhopal disaster|1984 chemical leak]] in Bhopal, India, which killed fifteen thousand people at the time and continues to have severe health effects on people who were in contact with the chemical cloud when the leak occurred.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Dixon |first=Guy |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/revisiting-the-bhopal-disaster/article18278398/ |title=Revisiting the Bhopal disaster |work=The Globe and Mail |location=Toronto, Ontario |date=December 2, 2004}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Forbidden Forest|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2005|01|06}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:Two men concerned about forestry policies on New Brunswick lands urge company officials and the New Brunswick government to practice responsible forestry, and they propose a new, community-based forestry policy – one that is environmentally sustainable and that produces more jobs than the highly capital-intensive, mechanized techniques being used.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://niche-canada.org/research/forest-history/forest-history-teaching-resources/ |title=Forest History: Teaching Resources |work=Network in Canadian History & Environment (NiCHE) |date=23 August 2010 |access-date=2016-05-18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Passion & Fury: The Emotional Brain: Anger |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2005|03|22}}|ProdCode=special |ShortSummary=
:Uncover the startling discoveries researchers make when they scan the brains of sociopaths and learn about the scientific studies that may answer the question: does biology dictate destiny? See how these studies are being put to use in helping young people cope with anger at an early age.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://curio.ca/en/video/passion-fury-the-emotional-brain-anger-part-1-of-4-863/ |title=Passion & Fury, The Emotional Brain: Anger (Part 1 of 4) |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |access-date=2016-05-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Passion & Fury: The Emotional Brain: Love |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2005|03|29}}|ProdCode=special |ShortSummary=
:Anthropologists dissect this emotion to its core: lust, romantic love and attachment. How we are aroused is explored: smell and tone of voice play into attraction and compatibility. Peel back the layers of the most profound expression of our humanity.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://curio.ca/en/video/passion-fury-the-emotional-brain-love-part-4-of-4-864/ |title=Passion & Fury, The Emotional Brain: Love (Part 4 of 4) |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |access-date=2016-05-21}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Passion & Fury: The Emotional Brain: Fear |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2005|05|11}} |ProdCode=special|ShortSummary=
:What triggers fear, our physiological reactions, and what purpose it serves are studied. Scientists investigate how our brain assesses the need for fight or flight.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://curio.ca/en/video/passion-fury-the-emotional-brain-fear-part-2-of-4-1319/ |title=Passion & Fury, The Emotional Brain: Fear (Part 2 of 4) |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |access-date=2016-05-21}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Passion & Fury: The Emotional Brain: Happiness |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2005|05|18}} |ProdCode=special |ShortSummary=
:Drawing a distinction between the lasting state of happiness and the pursuit of instant pleasure, the program explores the evolutionary role of happiness, and asks what happens in the brain, and possibly in our genes, that make some people happy and others sad.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=https://epl.bibliocommons.com/item/show/805114005 |title=The Nature of Things: Passion & Fury, the Emotional Brain. Happiness |work=Edmonton Public Library |publisher=BiblioCommons |access-date=2016-05-21}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Fighting Fire with Fire|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2005|05|26}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:''Fighting Fire with Fire'' raises questions about conventional methods of fighting fire, and whether decades of suppressing fire have simply made matters worse.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.bullfrogfilms.com/catalog/ffwf.html |title=Fighting Fire With Fire |work=Bullfrog Films, Inc. |access-date=2016-05-19 |archive-date=2016-05-18 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160518180414/http://www.bullfrogfilms.com/catalog/ffwf.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Being Caribou: Part 1|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2005|06|02}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:Hoping to raise awareness of the threat to the survival of the [[Porcupine caribou]] herd presented by the proposed exploitation of the oil and gas reserves in the [[Arctic National Wildlife Refuge]], a husband-and-wife-team follow the herd of 120,000 caribou on foot across 1,500 kilometers of rugged Arctic tundra.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.bullfrogfilms.com/catalog/bcar.html |title=Being Caribou |work=Bullfrog Films, Inc. |access-date=2016-05-19}}</ref><ref name="globe-being-caribou">{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Walmark |first=Henrietta |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/the-critical-list/article18228136/ |title=The Critical List |work=The Globe and Mail |location=Toronto, Ontario |date=May 28, 2005}}</ref>}}

