{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Secret Life of Pigeons|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|11|20}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:The filmmaker postulates that pigeons aren't an urban nuisance but an important cog in city life, to be studied, fancied, raced, and even honoured for their contributions to humanity.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Mele |first=Rick |url=http://www.huffingtonpost.ca/2014/11/18/secret-life-of-pigeons-nature-of-things_n_6180848.html |title='The Secret Life Of Pigeons' Review: Rebranding Nature's Most Misunderstood Bird |work=The Huffington Post Canada |date=2014-11-18 |access-date=2016-03-22}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Two of a Kind|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2014|11|27}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:A look at how twins are solving medical mysteries as researchers examine the differences between them.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/two-of-a-kind |title=Two of a Kind |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141107092942/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/two-of-a-kind |archive-date=2014-11-07 |access-date=2016-04-21}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Myth or Science 3: You Are What You Eat|OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2015|01|08}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:Health claims are put to the test, with evocative experiments that illustrate the results of more large-scale scientific studies about health.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|last=Buchner |first=Jill |url=http://www.canadianliving.com/blogs/health/2015/01/05/3-real-life-experiments-will-help-achieve-resolutions/ |title=3 real-life experiments that will help you achieve your resolutions |work=Canadian Living |date=January 5, 2015 |access-date=2016-04-21 |archive-date=2016-05-07 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160507164305/http://www.canadianliving.com/blogs/health/2015/01/05/3-real-life-experiments-will-help-achieve-resolutions/ |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Lion in Your Living Room|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|01|15}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:Explores intriguing theories of how the domesticated cat has evolved.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Ledger |first=Rebecca |url=https://vancouversun.com/life/Therapist+lion+your+living+room/10723396/story.html |title=Pet Therapist: The lion in your living room |work=The Vancouver Sun |date=January 14, 2015 |access-date=2016-03-27}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Mystery of the Monsoon|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|01|29}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:A cinematic exploration of the force of the monsoon and its unpredictability.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/mystery-of-the-moonsoon |title=The Mystery of the Monsoon |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150124023509/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/mystery-of-the-moonsoon |archive-date=2015-01-24 |access-date=2016-04-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Kung Fu Meerkats|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|02|05}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:''The Kung Fu Meerkats'' looks at the longest running animal behaviour study ever; a study in which young volunteer graduates and field scientists are trying to unravel the mysteries of meerkat behaviour.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-kung-fu-meerkats |title=The Kung Fu Meerkats |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150209013558/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-kung-fu-meerkats |archive-date=2015-02-09 |access-date=2016-04-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Human Odyssey: Rise of a Species |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2015|02|12}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:The filmmaker travels to southern Africa – the so-called cradle of civilization – to visit with a tribe of Kalahari bushmen whose lifestyle and hunting-gathering techniques are virtually identical to those employed by their ancestors thousands of years ago.<ref name="WFP-LongJourney">{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Oswald |first=Brad |url=http://www.winnipegfreepress.com/arts-and-life/entertainment/TV/come-along-for-the-ride-on-humanitys-long-journey-291495061.html |title=Come along for the ride on humanity's long journey |work=Winnipeg Free Press |date=2015-02-11 |access-date=2016-03-19}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Human Odyssey: The Adaptable Ape |OriginalAirDate= {{Start date|2015|02|19}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:This episode of ''The Great Human Odyssey'' looks at how humans migrated out of Africa, and onward to other lands.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/greathumanodyssey/episodes/episode-2-the-adaptable-ape |title=Episode 2: The Adaptable Ape |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150217215834/http://www.cbc.ca/greathumanodyssey/episodes/episode-2-the-adaptable-ape |archive-date=2015-02-17 |access-date=2016-04-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Great Human Odyssey: Journey's End|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|02|26}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
:The final installment investigates humanity's most audacious prehistoric trick: crossing the oceans to settle every major island and continent on Earth except Antarctica.<ref name="GlobeHumanOdyssey">{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Semeniuk |first=Ivan |url=https://www.theglobeandmail.com/arts/television/niobe-thompsons-cbc-series-dives-deep-into-the-origins-of-humankind/article23202497/ |title=Niobe Thompson's CBC series dives deep into the origins of humankind |work=The Globe and Mail |location=Canada |date=Feb 25, 2015 |access-date=2016-03-19}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=The Antibiotic Hunters|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|03|05}} |ProdCode=16 |ShortSummary=
:Medical experts around the world are alarmed by the rapid rise of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. This program looks at the causes of [[Antimicrobial resistance|antibiotic resistance]], and the efforts of researchers seeking to find new antibiotic substances by looking in a variety of exotic places.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Ellis |first=Erin |url=https://vancouversun.com/health/Next+generation+antibiotics+could+found+nature+says+from+Vancouver+filmmakers/10855178/story.html |title=Next generation of antibiotics could be found in nature, says new doc from Vancouver filmmakers |work=Vancouver Sun |date=March 1, 2015 |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150304050437/http://www.vancouversun.com/health/Next%2Bgeneration%2Bantibiotics%2Bcould%2Bfound%2Bnature%2Bsays%2Bfrom%2BVancouver%2Bfilmmakers/10855178/story.html |archive-date=March 4, 2015 |access-date=2016-04-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Safe Haven for Chimps|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|03|12}} |ProdCode=17 |ShortSummary=
:''Safe Haven for Chimps'' travels to the American deep south to [[Chimp Haven]] sanctuary to meet a special group of chimps, following a landmark decision in the U.S. to retire 300 federally-owned chimpanzees.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/safe-haven-for-chimps |title=Safe Haven for Chimps |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150302163637/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/safe-haven-for-chimps |archive-date=2015-03-02 |access-date=2016-04-20}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=SongbirdSOS|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|03|19}} |ProdCode=18 |ShortSummary=
:A documentary about songbird mass depletion and the compassionate people who are working to turn the tide.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Dunn |first=Scott |url=http://www.owensoundsuntimes.com/2015/11/15/songbird-documentary-inspired-by-the-bruce |title=Songbird documentary inspired by The Bruce |work=Owen Sound Sun Times |date=November 15, 2015 |access-date=2016-03-20 |archive-date=2016-04-05 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160405205041/http://www.owensoundsuntimes.com/2015/11/15/songbird-documentary-inspired-by-the-bruce |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Spirit Bear Family|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|03|26}} |ProdCode=19 |ShortSummary=
:''Spirit Bear Family'' features two black cubs of the year and their white mother, an especially feisty bear that isn't afraid to take on larger and more dangerous males.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|news|last=Pynn |first=Larry |url=https://vancouversun.com/Documentary+filmmaker+returns+film+white+Spirit+Bears+north+coast+years+later/10918965/story.html |title=Documentary filmmaker returns to film white Spirit Bears on B.C. north coast 25 years later |work=The Vancouver Sun |date=March 25, 2015 |access-date=2016-03-20 |archive-date=2016-05-06 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160506163951/http://www.vancouversun.com/Documentary+filmmaker+returns+film+white+Spirit+Bears+north+coast+years+later/10918965/story.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Jellyfish Rule!|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|04|02}} |ProdCode=20 |ShortSummary=
:A story about one of the planet's most successful animals. Jellyfish thrive in waters where virtually nothing else can, and their numbers are increasing in marine ecosystems around the globe. The proliferation of jellyfish raises an alarm about the condition of the oceans.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/jellyfish-rule |title=Jellyfish Rule! |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150328083923/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/jellyfish-rule |archive-date=2015-03-28 |access-date=2016-04-19}}</ref> Troublesome species featured in the program include [[Mnemiopsis]], [[Cannonball jellyfish]], and [[Nomura's jellyfish]].}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list|Title=Franklin's Lost Ships|OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2015|04|09}} |ProdCode=21 |ShortSummary=
:In 1845 [[John Franklin|Sir John Franklin]] set off to find the Northwest Passage through the Canadian Arctic. Franklin, his 2 ships and 129 men were never heard from again. ''Franklin's Lost Ships'' takes a look at the discovery of the expedition's flagship [[HMS Erebus (1826)|Erebus]].<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/franklins-lost-ships |title=Franklin's Lost Ships |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150414023020/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/franklins-lost-ships |archive-date=2015-04-14 |access-date=2016-04-19}}</ref>}}

