== Season 56: 2016–2017 ==
*Thursdays at 8:00 pm
*Length 1 hour
{| class="wikitable plainrowheaders" style="width:100%; margin:auto;"
!Title!!width="140"|Broadcast date!!width="40"|Episode

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Pompeii's People |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|10|06}} |ProdCode=01 |ShortSummary=
:The city of Pompeii was buried under the ash of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD, and then rediscovered 1,500 years later in the 18th century. Frozen in time by the ash, Pompeii presents a fascinating window into ancient Roman life, unlike anywhere else on earth.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/pompeiis-people |title=Pompeii's People |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160711125909/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/pompeiis-people |archive-date=2016-07-11 |access-date=2016-07-16}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Conversations with Dolphins |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|10|13}} |ProdCode=02 |ShortSummary=
:We know that dolphins are intelligent animals, but just how intelligent are they, and how is dolphin intelligence expressed? This film brings us to the research sites of some of the most internationally renowned dolphin specialists, and alongside experts studying dolphins in the wild.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/conversations-with-dolphins |title=Conversations with Dolphins |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160919185830/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/conversations-with-dolphins |archive-date=2016-09-19 |access-date=2016-09-19}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Running on Empty |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|10|20}} |ProdCode=03 |ShortSummary=
:As California siphons off massive amounts of water from its few remaining wetlands, its sluggish rivers and its aquifers, it faces a water supply that is dropping to an unprecedented low level. Suddenly, hard questions are being asked. Where did all the water go? Whose fault is it? And what needs to change to save California from its [[overconsumption]] of the available water resources?<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/running-on-empty |title=Running on Empty |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160711125804/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/running-on-empty |archive-date=2016-07-11 |access-date=2016-07-17}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=The Brain's Way of Healing |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|10|27}} |ProdCode=04 |ShortSummary=
:''The Brain's Way of Healing'' is about how science and medicine are looking at the brain in different ways and discovering that ailments and diseases can be dealt with by awakening the brain's own healing capacities.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-brains-way-of-healing |title=The Brain's Way of Healing |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160717204935/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-brains-way-of-healing |archive-date=2016-07-17 |access-date=2016-07-17}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Destination: Mars |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|11|03}} |ProdCode=05 |ShortSummary=
:The race to Mars is happening now. Billions are being spent. New generations rockets are being tested. Questions are being asked. How will we get there? How might we settle there? How would we try to transform Mars? Should we?<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/destination-mars |title=Destination: Mars |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161008000211/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/destination-mars |archive-date=2016-10-08 |access-date=2016-10-07}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Vital Bonds |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|11|17}} |ProdCode=06 |ShortSummary=
:A look inside the evolving science of transplants, where breakthrough discoveries are tackling the organ shortage and transforming the future of medicine.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/vital-bonds |title=Vital Bonds |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161017234053/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/vital-bonds |archive-date=2016-10-17 |access-date=2016-10-17}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Think Like An Animal |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|11|24}} |ProdCode=07 |ShortSummary=
:How smart are animals? Traditionally, we've answered that question by comparing them to us. A psychology professor presents a different approach, in which intelligence is all about what an animal needs to know in order to survive in its world, not ours. ''Think Like an Animal'' provides a glimpse into the inner lives of animals and challenges conventional wisdom, raising important questions about how we humans think about and treat our fellow creatures.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/think-like-an-animal |title=Think Like An Animal |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161023001350/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/think-like-an-animal |archive-date=2016-10-23 |access-date=2016-10-23}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=I Got Rhythm: The Science of Song |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2016|12|01}} |ProdCode=08 |ShortSummary=
:Exploring the science in music and humans. Scientists and archaeologists study human need for music and various effects that music has on people.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/i-got-rhythm-the-science-of-song |title=I Got Rhythm: The Science of Song |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161105173356/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/i-got-rhythm-the-science-of-song |archive-date=2016-11-05 |access-date=2016-11-05}}</ref>}}

<!-- 12/22/2016: No episode – Movie – A Christmas Story -->
<!-- 12/29/2016: No episode – Movie – Mr. Popper's Penguins -->
<!-- 01/05/2017: Repeat – The Curious Case of Vitamins and Me -->

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=The Secret Life of Owls |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|01|12}} |ProdCode=09 |ShortSummary=
:Learn about owls in this visit with owl experts and owls in the wild and in captivity.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-secret-life-of-owls |title=The Secret Life of Owls |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161201192842/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-secret-life-of-owls |archive-date=2016-12-01 |access-date=2016-12-01}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=PTSD: Beyond Trauma |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|01|19}} |ProdCode=10 |ShortSummary=
:''PTSD: Beyond Trauma'' follows researchers and people living with PTSD as they look for answers. Unfolding discoveries raise key questions about the fault-lines of fear and memory, and the roles geography and early development may play in predicting personal responses to trauma.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/ptsd-beyond-trauma |title=PTSD: Beyond Trauma |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161215032725/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/ptsd-beyond-trauma |archive-date=2016-12-15 |access-date=2016-12-15}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Myth or Science: The Secrets of Our Senses |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|01|26}} |ProdCode=11 |ShortSummary=
:Dr. Jennifer Gardy returns to CBC's ''The Nature of Things'' with the fifth in the very popular Myth or Science series. In this episode Gardy meets an unusual group of scientists who are rewriting our understanding of how the senses really work. Unique and sometimes bizarre experiments are used to trick our brain into revealing the secrets of our senses.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/myth-or-science-the-secrets-of-our-senses |title=Myth or Science: The Secrets of Our Senses |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161222153230/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/myth-or-science-the-secrets-of-our-senses |archive-date=2016-12-22 |access-date=2016-12-23}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=The Great Wild Indoors |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|02|09}} |ProdCode=12 |ShortSummary=
:A team of young entomologists are doing a curious kind of fieldwork: entering homes on every continent to do an inventory of the mini-fauna they find. The indoor biome is a world of insects and arachnids as rich, as surprising and as beautiful as any other ecosystem.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-great-wild-indoors |title=The Great Wild Indoors |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161217044210/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/the-great-wild-indoors |archive-date=2016-12-17 |access-date=2016-12-18}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Body Language Decoded |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|02|16}} |ProdCode=13 |ShortSummary=
:''Body Language Decoded'' explores the science behind one of the oldest forms of human communication, and reveals how researchers are unraveling its secrets in unexpected – and often surprising – ways.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/body-language-decoded |title=Body Language Decoded |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170119045755/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/body-language-decoded |archive-date=2017-01-19 |access-date=2017-01-19}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Cracking Cancer |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|02|23}} |ProdCode=14 |ShortSummary=
:''Cracking Cancer'' follows a group of patients, all with incurable cancer, through a highly experimental clinical trial at the [[BC Cancer Agency]]. The trial compares patients' normal DNA with that of their tumours, to find the genetic mutations causing their cancer. The research team then searches to find which drug, of all available drugs, might block that growth factor. The results have been quite promising.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/cracking-cancer |title=Cracking Cancer |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170124224015/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/cracking-cancer |archive-date=2017-01-24 |access-date=2017-01-24}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Dad and the Dandelions |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|03|02}} |ProdCode=15 |ShortSummary=
:A filmmaker searches for the origin of the cancer which took the life of his father. Among the possibilities, he looks at the use of lawn pesticides on golf courses as a possible factor leading to cancers, and explores environmentally friendly alternatives for golf course maintenance.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/dad-and-the-dandelions |title=Dad and the Dandelions |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170210032331/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/dad-and-the-dandelions |archive-date=2017-02-10 |access-date=2017-02-10}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=White Wolves: Ghosts of the Arctic |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|03|09}} |ProdCode=16 |ShortSummary=
:''Ghosts of the Arctic'' follows a family of wolves as they struggle to raise their pups in Canada's northern arctic wilderness.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/white-wolves-ghosts-of-the-arctic |title=White Wolves: Ghosts of the Arctic |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170208190817/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/white-wolves-ghosts-of-the-arctic |archive-date=2017-02-08 |access-date=2017-02-08}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=ADHD: Not Just For Kids |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|03|16}} |ProdCode=17 |ShortSummary=
:''ADHD: Not Just For Kids'' aims to dispel the myths and stigmas about [[attention deficit hyperactivity disorder]], a condition that many people, kids and adults alike, often live with for years, unrecognized or misdiagnosed.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/adhd-not-just-for-kids |title=ADHD: Not Just For Kids |work=CBC/Radio-Canada |url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170303182443/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/adhd-not-just-for-kids |archive-date=2017-03-03 |access-date=2017-03-03}}</ref>}}

{{#invoke:Episode list|list |Title=Fox Tales |OriginalAirDate={{Start date|2017|03|23}} |ProdCode=18 |ShortSummary=
:''Fox Tales'' presents new research that closely examines the first hours, days, and weeks of a red fox pup's life.<ref>{{#invoke:cite|web|title=Fox Tales|url=http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/fox-tales|website=CBC/Radio-Canada|url-status = live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170304033041/http://www.cbc.ca/natureofthings/episodes/fox-tales |archive-date=2017-03-04 |access-date=2017-03-04}}</ref>}}
